<DocsLayout title="Quick Start">
  <h1>Quick Start</h1>
  <p>Boot Identity, hit the health endpoint, sign up a user, log them back in. About five minutes.</p>

  <h2>1. Start an EntDB instance</h2>
  <p>Identity needs an EntDB endpoint. The simplest option is a dev container:</p>
  <pre is:raw><code class="language-bash">docker run -d --name entdb -p 50051:50051 \
  ghcr.io/elloloop/entdb:latest</code></pre>

  <h2>2. Run Identity</h2>
  <p>The image is published to GitHub Container Registry. The minimum env you need is the EntDB address and a tenant ID.</p>
  <pre is:raw><code class="language-bash">docker run -d --name identity \
  -p 80:80 -p 9090:9090 \
  -e GATEWAY_ENTDB_ADDRESS=host.docker.internal:50051 \
  -e GATEWAY_DEFAULT_TENANT_ID=quickstart \
  -e GATEWAY_AUTH_ALLOW_LOCAL=true \
  ghcr.io/elloloop/identity:latest</code></pre>

  <Callout type="warning" title="Dev mode only">
    With <code>GATEWAY_JWT_KEYS</code> unset, Identity auto-generates an ephemeral RSA signing key on boot and logs <code>auto_generating_dev_rsa_key</code>. That's fine for local work but useless in production &mdash; tokens become invalid the moment the container restarts. See <a href="/identity/docs/installation/jwt-keys">JWT Keys</a> for the prod setup.
  </Callout>

  <h2>3. Health check</h2>
  <pre is:raw><code class="language-bash">curl -s http://localhost/health
# {"status":"ok"}</code></pre>

  <h2>4. Sign up a user</h2>
  <p>Connect-RPC speaks plain HTTP/JSON. The URL is the proto service path; the body is a JSON-encoded request message.</p>
  <pre is:raw><code class="language-bash">curl -s -X POST http://localhost/identity.IdentityService/PasswordSignup \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "email": "alice@example.com",
    "password": "Sup3rStrong!Pass",
    "recovery_email": "alice-backup@example.com"
  }'</code></pre>
  <p>Response:</p>
  <pre is:raw><code class="language-json">{
  "user": {
    "id": "user_01HX...",
    "email": "alice@example.com",
    "name": "alice",
    "role": "member",
    "status": "USER_STATUS_ACTIVE"
  },
  "accessToken": "eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiIsImtpZCI6ImRldiIs...",
  "refreshToken": "rt_...",
  "expiresIn": 900
}</code></pre>

  <h2>5. Log in</h2>
  <pre is:raw><code class="language-bash">curl -s -X POST http://localhost/identity.IdentityService/PasswordLogin \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"email":"alice@example.com","password":"Sup3rStrong!Pass"}'</code></pre>
  <p>You'll get back a fresh <code>access_token</code> and a rotated <code>refresh_token</code>. The access token lasts 15 minutes by default; the refresh token lasts seven days.</p>

  <h2>6. Hit an authenticated RPC</h2>
  <pre is:raw><code class="language-bash">ACCESS=&quot;eyJ...&quot;  # paste from step 5
curl -s -X POST http://localhost/identity.IdentityService/GetCurrentUser \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $ACCESS" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{}'</code></pre>

  <h2>7. Inspect the JWKS</h2>
  <p>Downstream services verify tokens using the public keys at:</p>
  <pre is:raw><code class="language-bash">curl -s http://localhost/.well-known/jwks.json | jq .</code></pre>
  <p>That's the contract: any service in your stack can fetch this once, cache it, and verify Identity-issued tokens locally.</p>

